The numbers $1,10,10^{2}, \ldots, 10^{n-1}$ provide an example of $n$ different numbers from which the maximum number of different sums can be formed. The sum of any $k$ numbers from this set is a number whose decimal representation uses only 1 and 0. Each such sum can be represented as an $n$-element ordered set of 0s and 1s. Since each position in the set can have only two digits, their total number is $2 \cdot 2 \cdot \ldots \cdot 2=2^{n}$. The only impossible set, consisting of $n$ zeros, must be excluded, so the total number of valid sets is $2^{n}-1$.

