10. (3 points) The teachers and students of a school donated a total of 1995 yuan to a poor area. The school has a total of 35 teachers and 14 teaching classes, with each class having the same number of students, more than 30 but no more than 45. If the average amount of money donated per person is an integer, then the average amount donated per person is $\qquad$ 3 yuan.

【Solution】If each class has 30 students, then the number of people who donated is $30 \times 14 + 35 = 455$ people; if each class has 45 students, then the number of people who donated is: $45 \times 14 + 35 = 665$ people;
$$
1995 = 3 \times 5 \times 7 \times 19 ;
$$

Among the factors, only $5 \times 7 \times 19 = 665$;
meets the requirement, meaning a total of 665 people donated.
$$
1995 \div 665 = 3 \text { (yuan); }
$$

Answer: The average amount donated per person is 3 yuan.
Therefore, the answer is: 3 .

I. solution. Let us denote the required sum by $S_{n}$. For $n=1,2,3$, and 4, we have

$$
S_{1}=\frac{1}{3}, \quad S_{2}=\frac{11}{24}, \quad S_{3}=\frac{59}{120}, \quad S_{4}=\frac{359}{720}
$$

In the denominators of the last three values, 4!, 5!, and 6! appear, respectively, and their numerators are one less than half of the denominator. This regularity also holds for $S_{1}$ in the form of $2 / 6$. Therefore, these sums are of the form

$$
S_{n}=\frac{\frac{(n+2)!}{2}-1}{(n+2)!}=\frac{1}{2}-\frac{1}{(n+2)!}
$$

We will prove by induction that this relationship holds for all natural numbers $n$.

As a preparatory step, we simplify the $k$-th term of $S_{n}$, where $1 \leqq k \leqq n$. By factoring out $k!$ in the denominator, then further transforming and simplifying the fraction by $k+2$,

$$
\begin{gathered}
\frac{k+2}{k!+(k+1)!+(k+2)!}= \\
\frac{(k+2)}{k!(1+k+1+(k+1)(k+2))}=\frac{k+2}{k!(k+2)^{2}}=\frac{1}{k!(k+2)}
\end{gathered}
$$

Assume that (1) holds for some index $n$, we will show that it also holds for the index one greater. Using (2),

$$
\begin{aligned}
S_{n+1} & =S_{n}+\frac{1}{(n+1)!(n+3)}=\frac{1}{2}-\left(\frac{1}{(n+2)!}-\frac{1}{(n+1)!(n+3)}\right)= \\
& =\frac{1}{2}-\frac{n+3-(n+2)}{(n+3)!}=\frac{1}{2}-\frac{1}{(n+3)!}=\frac{1}{2}-\frac{1}{((n+1)+2)!}
\end{aligned}
$$

This follows from (1) by substituting $n+1$ for $n$. Thus, (1) is indeed true for all natural numbers. In a one-term expression form,

$$
S_{n}=\frac{(n+2)!-2}{2(n+2)!}
$$

Szeidl László (Budapest, Apáczai Csere J. high school III. grade)

II. solution. The above (2) expression can also be written as:

$$
\begin{gathered}
\frac{k+2}{k!+(k+1)!+(k+2)!}=\frac{1}{k!(k+2)}=\frac{k+1}{(k+2)!}=\frac{k+2-1}{(k+2)!}= \\
=\frac{1}{(k+1)!}-\frac{1}{(k+2)!}
\end{gathered}
$$

Transforming each term of $S_{n}$ in this way, we arrive at the second form of (1):

$$
\begin{aligned}
S_{n}=( & \left.\frac{1}{2!}-\frac{1}{3!}\right)+\left(\frac{1}{3!}-\frac{1}{4!}\right)+\ldots+\left(\frac{1}{n!}-\frac{1}{(n+1)!}\right)+ \\
& +\left(\frac{1}{(n+1)!}-\frac{1}{(n+2)!}\right)=\frac{1}{2!}-\frac{1}{(n+2)!}
\end{aligned}
$$

Hernádi Ágnes (Budapest, Berzsenyi D. high school I. grade)

Remark. Essentially, we arrive at the same solution if we add the consecutive terms of the sum

$$
S_{n}^{*}=\frac{1}{3!}+\frac{1}{4!}+\ldots+\frac{1}{n+2!}
$$

to each term of $S_{n}$ and notice that the sum, based on (2),

$$
\begin{gathered}
\frac{1}{k!(k+2)}+\frac{1}{(k+2)!}=\frac{1}{(k+1)!}, \quad \text { so the total sum is } \\
\frac{1}{2!}+S_{n}^{*}-\frac{1}{(n+2)!}
\end{gathered}
$$

